Welcome to BirdCLEF+ 2026 - Meet the hosts
BirdCLEF+ offers an exciting opportunity to showcase your skills while contributing to global conservation efforts. This year’s challenge is especially relevant for conservation initiatives in the Pantanal, where climate change and habitat loss continue to threaten biodiversity.

As your competition hosts, we’re here to support you as much as possible—while also keeping the integrity of the test data intact. So while we’re happy to answer questions, we won’t be revealing any secrets about the dataset! 😉

Now, let’s introduce the team:

@stefankahl – I’m a research associate at the K. Lisa Yang Center for Conservation Bioacoustics (Cornell Lab of Ornithology) and Chemnitz University of Technology. My work focuses on developing machine learning models to detect and identify bird species in large-scale audio datasets. I also serve as the technology lead for the BirdNET project and have been organizing the BirdCLEF Challenge since 2018. Feel free to ask me about deep learning for bioacoustics or working note submissions.

@tomdenton – A research software engineer at Google and long-time co-host of the BirdCLEF Kaggle competitions. His team works on organizing natural soundscapes in ways that support conservation—essentially tackling key ML challenges so ecologists can focus on scientific questions rather than annotating data and training classifiers. You can explore some of his past work on birdsong audio separation and source-free domain adaptation. Tom is happy to help with questions about cutting-edge ML research.

@larissasugai – An ecologist passionate about using acoustic monitoring to understand biodiversity changes across space and time. Her research has focused on signaling assemblages in South America, particularly frogs. At the Cornell Lab, Larissa works with the K. Lisa Yang Center for Conservation Bioacoustics as part of the capacity building team. She can help answer questions about the Pantanal and its ecological challenges.

@holgerklinck – Director of the K. Lisa Yang Center for Conservation Bioacoustics at the Cornell Lab of Ornithology. Holger leads efforts to develop and apply innovative conservation technologies to record and analyze sounds in nature at ecologically meaningful scales. He can provide insights on bioacoustic monitoring and conservation technology.

@ryanholbrook & @ashleyoldacre – Representing the Kaggle team, Ashley & Ryan are here to assist with competition rules, platform-related issues, and anything Kaggle-specific.

We’re excited to see what you’ll build—happy Kaggling! 🚀


